{
  "dom_id": "step-q344343",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/df853db764aacccd603bafdfb8c8716e53098620a7f5857b0236203437b217d0.png)\n\nFind the area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ of the largest rectangle that can be inscribed in the closed region bounded by the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the graph of $y=4-2x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=4-2x.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(4-2x)=4x-2{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=1$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=1$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(1) & =4(1)-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
